<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- What the App binary reaches for that Apple asks a reason for.

     WITHOUT THIS FILE THE DELIVERY IS REFUSED, AND NOT BY A RED TICK. The
     upload succeeds, the workflow goes green, and an hour later an email
     arrives (ITMS-91053, "Missing API declaration"). That is the same shape
     as build 86, which went green and came back as ITMS-90158 for a
     placeholder URL scheme — the one class of failure nothing in this
     repository can watch happen.

     NONE OF OUR OWN SWIFT REACHES FOR ONE. Measured, not assumed: the four
     files under ios/App/App, the six under LinguaKeyboard and the eight under
     LinguaWidget name no UserDefaults, no file timestamp, no boot time, no
     disk space and no active-keyboard API. The App Group is reached by
     FileManager.containerURL and read as a file, which is on none of Apple's
     lists.

     IT IS A POD THAT REACHES FOR ONE. @capgo/capacitor-social-login is
     compiled into the App binary (the Podfile's capacitor_pods, target 'App')
     and keeps the signed-in name in UserDefaults.standard — AppleProvider
     writes it on sign-in and reads it back, and three more of its providers do
     the same with their own keys. That pod ships NO privacy manifest of its
     own, which is what makes the gap easy to miss: Capacitor's two pods DO
     ship one, so the folder looks covered.

       find node_modules/@capgo/capacitor-social-login -iname '*.xcprivacy'   0
       find node_modules/@capacitor/ios               -iname '*.xcprivacy'   2

     So the declaration has nowhere else to live and has to be the app's own.
     `assets-check` derives that requirement rather than restating it: it
     re-reads the pods on every run, and if this pod ever ships its own
     manifest, or a new pod reaches for a category not declared here, the
     check says so.

     WHAT THIS CANNOT SEE. GoogleSignIn and Alamofire arrive through
     CocoaPods on the build machine, not through node_modules, so nothing in
     this repository can read them. Each carries its own manifest and that is
     its vendor's to keep; if Apple's mail ever names one of those, it is
     answered by moving that pod's version, not by adding a line here.

     CA92.1 is Apple's reason for reading and writing preferences that only
     this app can see. That is what the pod does: it writes under
     UserDefaults.standard, which no other app can read, and reads its own
     keys back. It is not 1C8F.1 — nothing here puts a preference in an App
     Group — and not C56D.1, which is the reason an SDK declares in its OWN
     manifest rather than one an app declares on an SDK's behalf.

     NSPrivacyTracking IS false AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT. The Facebook
     provider is the only file in that pod that names AppTrackingTransparency,
     and its whole class sits inside `#if canImport(FBSDKLoginKit)`; facebook
     is false in capacitor.config.json, so the plugin's own sync hook comments
     the FBSDK pods out of its podspec and that class is never compiled. No
     other line under ios/ or node_modules names ATTrackingManager or the
     advertising identifier.

     NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and absent is not the
     same as empty. An empty array is a claim that this app collects nothing,
     which is untrue: it holds an address, a handle, a name, a line somebody
     wrote about themselves, photographs, a voice, what people searched for
     and what they wrote. Where that is answered is App Store Connect's
     privacy questionnaire — the owner's screen, listed item by item in
     docs/apple.md § 5. Answering it here as well would be the same list in
     two places, free to drift, which is what this repository calls one place
     not fifteen.

     A FILE ON DISK IS NOT A FILE IN THE APP. It has to be in the App target's
     Resources build phase, exactly the way a .swift has to be in Sources —
     Compose.swift was written, committed, pushed, left out of the Sources
     phase, and the build failed naming a missing TYPE rather than a missing
     file. This one is worse: leaving it out of Resources fails nothing at
     all. The archive is green, the upload is green, and the mail arrives an
     hour later. `assets-check` holds both halves. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
